    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Grade:</b> 10th Grade (CEFR: B1.1)</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Scenario:</b> Study Habits for Success</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Theme:</b> How I Learned to Focus</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Productive: Describe familiar processes or events. Interactive: Paraphrase a simple statement on familiar topics during discussions.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can describe a personal study routine, outlining steps in logical order and giving specific examples (e.g., "First, I organize my notes by subject. Then, I review my math exercises. Finally, I test myself with flashcards."). Can paraphrase feedback on study habits during a discussion (e.g., "So you're saying that I have changed my study habits?"). Can suggest study habit improvements based on past experiences and provide reasons for their effectiveness (e.g., "I used to forget assignments, so I started using a planner. Now, I submit my work on time and feel less stressed.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

## SECTION 3 — AOA STAGES TABLE
<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #e6e6e6;">
            <th style="padding: 10px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="padding: 10px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 47%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="padding: 10px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 20%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays a series of images showing different study environments (e.g., messy desk, organized desk, student distracted by phone, student focused). Students briefly discuss in pairs: "Which study environment do you think is most effective for focusing? What is one study habit you used to have that was not effective?"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models the target language for discussing past habits and achievements in study routines. For example, the teacher says: "I used to procrastinate a lot with my assignments, but since high school, I have developed better time management skills. Now, I have a consistent schedule, and I feel less stressed. I have organized my study space." The teacher emphasizes the pronunciation of "-ed" endings in 'developed,' 'organized,' 'stressed,' and stresses the main verb in 'have developed'.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of the modeled phrases with Concept Checking Questions:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I say 'I used to procrastinate,' am I doing it now or did I do it in the past?" (Past)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When I say 'I have developed better habits,' does that mean my habits are good now, or were they always good?" (They are good now/have improved)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Study Habits for Success".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short dialogue (e.g., 2-3 minutes long) between two high school students discussing how they have improved their study habits. The dialogue incorporates the target grammar (Present Perfect for achievements, Past Simple for past habits, WH-questions) and vocabulary (e.g., 'procrastinate,' 'manage time,' 'focus,' 'develop strategies,' 'achieve goals,' 'improve grades'). The register is informal, conversational.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Example Dialogue Excerpt:</b><br/>
                    <b>Ana:</b> "Hey Carlos, you look really focused these days. What have you done to change your study routine? I remember you used to get distracted easily."<br/>
                    <b>Carlos:</b> "Yeah, I have definitely developed better habits. I used to procrastinate on big assignments, which always affected my grades. But now, I have learned to organize my time effectively and set clear goals. I've also found that taking short breaks helps me maintain focus."<br/>
                    <b>Ana:</b> "That's inspiring! So, how have these new strategies improved your learning process? What specific steps have you taken?"
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Organize group projects that promote collaborative discussions on effective study strategies, emphasizing how language is used to negotiate plans and share experiences, while providing concrete examples of time management techniques adapted to the group's cultural and social norms.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> Students listen to or read the dialogue and answer gist questions orally:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What are Ana and Carlos discussing?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What is one thing Carlos used to struggle with?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What is one new habit Carlos has developed?</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Speaking.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice expressing achievements in changing habits (Present Perfect: "I have developed," "I have reorganized") and past habits (Past Simple: "I used to procrastinate"). Practice WH-questions ("What have you done to change...?"), and using vocabulary related to study habits (e.g., 'manage,' 'procrastinate,' 'improve,' 'develop,' 'reflect,' 'set goals,' 'organize,' 'accomplish,' 'effective,' 'consistent'). Pronunciation focus on /sk/ in "skill," /st/ in "study," /s/ in "success," /t/ in "type", and -ed endings (e.g., /d/ in "improved," /t/ in "accomplished," /ɪd/ in "suggested"), stressing the main verb in present perfect forms.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Sentence Completion (Controlled Practice):</b> Students work in pairs. The teacher provides a list of incomplete sentences on the board or a handout. Students must complete each sentence orally using "I used to..." or "I have [past participle]..." and a relevant vocabulary word. They practice the pronunciation of the -ed endings and main verb stress.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example: "Before, I _______ (forget) my assignments, but now I _______ (organize) them better." (Expected: "I *used to forget* my assignments, but now I *have organized* them better.")</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example: "My grades _______ (suffer) because I didn't _______ (focus) in class, but recently I _______ (improve) my concentration." (Expected: "My grades *used to suffer* because I didn't *focus* in class, but recently I *have improved* my concentration.")</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Habit Transformation Chain Drill (Semi-Controlled Practice):</b> The teacher starts a sentence: "I used to [bad habit verb], but now I have [good habit past participle]." Student A repeats the sentence and adds their own. Student B then repeats Student A's sentence and adds their own, creating a chain. This encourages quick thinking and accurate structure use.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher: "I used to procrastinate, but now I have developed effective time management skills."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student A: "I used to procrastinate, but now I have developed effective time management skills. I used to get easily distracted, but now I have improved my focus during study time."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student B: "I used to get easily distracted, but now I have improved my focus during study time. I used to cram for tests, but now I have started to review my notes every day."</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Interview Your Peer: Study Changes (Pair Interaction/Peer Check):</b> In pairs, students interview each other using WH-questions related to study habit changes. They must paraphrase at least one of their partner's responses to ensure understanding and demonstrate interactive speaking.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student A: "What have you done to improve your study habits this year?"</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student B: "I have started to prioritize my assignments and set realistic goals. I used to feel overwhelmed, but now I feel more in control."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student A (paraphrasing): "So, you're saying that by setting goals and prioritizing, you have managed to reduce your stress and feel more productive?"</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Study Habits for Success."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format: "My Study Habits Transformation Pitch and Peer Suggestion"</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students work in small groups of 3-4. Each student takes turns performing a short "pitch" (1-2 minutes) about their personal journey of improving study habits. The pitch should:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Describe their current personal study routine, outlining steps in logical order, giving specific examples (e.g., "First, I organize my notes by subject. Then, I review my math exercises. Finally, I test myself with flashcards.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Reflect on a past study habit they "used to" have that was ineffective, and explain how they "have developed" or "have improved" it (e.g., "I used to forget assignments, so I started using a planner. Now, I submit my work on time and feel less stressed.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">After each pitch, another group member must paraphrase one key idea or change mentioned by the speaker (e.g., "So you're saying that you have changed your study habits by incorporating regular breaks?").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Then, based on their own experiences and common effective strategies, each student suggests a study habit improvement for a *hypothetical* peer struggling with focus or time management, providing reasons for its effectiveness. They should refer to techniques adapted to cultural or social norms (e.g., "For someone who used to struggle with focusing during long study sessions, I would suggest trying the Pomodoro technique. We should use this technique where we study for 25 minutes, then take a 5-minute break. This helps us stay focused without feeling overwhelmed and can be adapted for our group projects.")</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The teacher circulates, monitoring for fluent use of target grammar and vocabulary, pronunciation, and effective collaborative discussion.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy and student self-assessment.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist focusing on speaking criteria during the "My Study Habits Transformation Pitch and Peer Suggestion" task.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students perform the group discussion from Stage 4.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation, especially of contrastive sounds (/s/, /t/, /sk/, /st/) and -ed endings.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Appropriate and accurate use of Present Perfect for achievements (e.g., "I have developed," "I have reorganized") and Past Simple for past habits (e.g., "I used to procrastinate").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Effective use of target vocabulary (e.g., 'manage,' 'procrastinate,' 'improve,' 'focus,' 'achieve,' 'organize,' 'effective,' 'consistent,' 'productivity').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to describe a personal study routine, outlining steps in logical order with specific examples.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to paraphrase a peer's statement during discussion.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to suggest study habit improvements and provide clear reasons for their effectiveness.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Active and collaborative interaction within the group.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete the following prompts individually:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One new thing I have learned about my study habits or effective strategies today is..."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I feel confident/somewhat confident/not confident about describing my study routine and improvements in English because..."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Student self-reflection and teacher reflection for future lessons.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection Prompts:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What specific changes have I made to my study habits that have led to success, and how can I explain them clearly to others using the phrases 'I used to...' and 'I have developed...'?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How did paraphrasing help me understand my classmates' study strategies today, and how can I use this skill to improve communication in other discussions?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What aspects of the speaking activities worked best to encourage active participation and accurate use of target grammar (Present Perfect, Past Simple) and vocabulary?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What challenges did students face in describing their routines, paraphrasing, or suggesting improvements? What adjustments or scaffolds (e.g., sentence starters, visual aids) are needed for Lesson 4, which will focus on writing?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson focused on *speaking* about our improved study habits and strategies. In Lesson 4, we will transition to *writing* about these topics, constructing paragraphs and short essays that describe our personal study routines and suggested improvements, applying the vocabulary and grammar practiced today into written compositions.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>